          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em Euclidean space
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            and specifically
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+geometry"} Euclidean geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is a fomalization in modern terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            studied in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Euclid300BC"} Euclid 300BC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} structures
            that Euclid recognised his spaces as having.
          %p
            In the strict sense of the word, Euclidean space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^n
            of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is, up to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometry"} isometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            whose underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distance"} distance
            function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            is given by the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+norm"} Euclidean norm
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi Eucl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo =
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %munderover
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi i
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d_{Eucl}(x,y) \coloneqq {\Vert x-y\Vert} = \sqrt{ \sum_{i = 1}^n (y_i - x_i)^2 }
                  \\,.
          %p
            In
            %a{:href => "#Euclid300BC"} Euclid 300BC
            this is considered for
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+functions"} coordinate functions
            as above, but via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+geometry"} synthetic geometry
          %p
            This means that in a Euclidean space one may construct for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+sphere"} unit sphere
            around any point, or the shortest
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curve"} curve
            connecting any two points. These are the operations studied in (
            %a{:href => "#Euclid300BC"}> Euclid 300BC
            ), see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+geometry"} Euclidean geometry
          %p
            Of course these operations may be considered in
            %em every
            (other)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-Euclidean+geometry"} non-Euclidean geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+geometry"} Euclidean geometry
            is distinguished notably from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+geometry"} elliptic geometry
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperbolic+geometry"} hyperbolic geometry
            by the fact that it satisfies the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+postulate"} parallel postulate
          %p
            In regarding
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi Eucl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^n = (\mathbb{R}^n, d_{Eucl})
            (only) as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            still carried by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            is disregarded, such as its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            structure, hence its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
            structure and its canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product+space"} inner product space
            structure. Sometimes “Euclidean space” is used to refer to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^n
            with that further extra structure remembered, which might then be called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
          %p
            Retaining the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
            on top of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
            structure means that on top of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distances"} distances
            one may also speak of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/angles"} angles
            in a Euclidean space.
          %p
            Then of course
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            carries also non-canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product+space"} inner product space
            structures, not corresponding to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+norm"} Euclidean norm
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^n
            as equipped with these one says that it is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong pseudo-Euclidean space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+geometry"} pseudo-Riemannian geometry
          %p
            Finally one could generalize and allow the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            to be countably infinite, and regard separable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            as generalized Euclidean spaces.
          %h2#remarks_on_terminology Remarks on terminology
          %p
            Arguably, the spaces studied by Euclid were not really modelled on inner product spaces, as the distances were lengths, not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            (which, if non-negative, are
            %em ratios
            of lengths). So we should say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            has an inner product valued in some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line"} line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            (or rather, in
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
          %p
            Since the days of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ren%C3%A9+Descartes"} René Descartes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            the dimension. But Euclid's spaces had no coordinates; and in any case, what we do with them is still coordinate-independent.
          %h2#euclidean_spaces_with_infinitesimals Euclidean spaces with infinitesimals
          %p
            Instead of working in the real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            and
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            real vector spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+Artinian+local+ring"}
              Archimedean ordered Artinian local
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rank"} rank
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            has
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimals"} infinitesimals
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimals"} infinitesimals
            as well. Nevertheless, it is still possible to define the Euclidean distance function on
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudometric"} pseudometric
            rather than a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
            here.
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"} local ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            by its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
            of non-invertible elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            itself, and the canonical function used in defining the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+ring"} quotient ring
            is the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re:A \to \mathbb{R}
            which takes a number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            to its purely real component
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℜ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Re(a) \in \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an ordered
            = succeed "-algebra," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strictly+monotone"} strictly monotone
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+homomorphism"} ring homomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:\mathbb{R} \to A
          %p
            The real numbers have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
            structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi min
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \min:\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi max
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \max:\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            has a distance function given by the function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho:A \times A \to \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi max
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi min
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℜ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(a, b) \coloneqq \max(\Re(a), \Re(b)) - \min(\Re(a), \Re(b))
          %p
            as well as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+value"} absolute value
            given by the function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert-\vert:A \to \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert a \vert \coloneqq \rho(a, 0)
          %p
            Since
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi min
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi max
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \min(a, b) \leq \max(a, b)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudometric"} pseudometric
            and multiplicative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/seminorm"} seminorm
            are always non-negative. In addition, by definition, the pseudometric takes any two elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in A
            whose difference
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo −
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a - b \in I
            is an infinitesimal to zero
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(a, b) = 0
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+field"} Euclidean field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+square+root+function"} metric square root function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msqrt
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqrt{-}:[0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rank"} rank
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            with basis
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo :
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Fin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v:\mathrm{Fin}(n) \to V
            thus has a
            %strong Euclidean pseudometric
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi V
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_V:V \times V \to K
            defined by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Fin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ρ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi b
                        %mi i
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_V(a, b) \coloneqq \sqrt{\sum_{i \in \mathrm{Fin}(n)} \rho(a_i, b_i)^2}
          %p
            for module elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in V
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \in V
            and scalars
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i \in A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_i \in A
            for index
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Fin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in \mathrm{Fin}(n)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Fin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Fin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = \sum_{i \in \mathrm{Fin}(n)} a_i v_i \quad b = \sum_{i \in \mathrm{Fin}(n)} b_i v_i
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+field"} ordered field
          %h2#in_constructive_mathematics In constructive mathematics
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            used to define Euclidean spaces are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_{D}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+complete"} Dedekind complete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+linear+order"} dense linear order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape"} shape
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ʃ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi 𝟙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \esh(\mathbb{R}_D) \cong \mathbb{1}
          %h3#in_predicative_constructive_mathematics In predicative constructive mathematics
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            constructive mathematics, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            are defined relative to a universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_\mathcal{U}
            for each
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_\mathcal{U}
            would be large relative to the sets in the universe
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
          %p
            If the predicative constructive foundations does not have universes, then there doesn’t exist any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+linear+order"} dense linear order
            that is actually
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+complete"} Dedekind complete
            in the usual sense, and so the usual definition of Euclidean space does not work. Some mathematicians have proposed to use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+space"} Sierpinski space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-frame"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
                \-frame
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            in a universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                      %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}_\mathcal{U}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+Dedekind+complete"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
                \-Dedekind complete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+complete"} Dedekind complete
            = succeed "-frames" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^{'}
            that embed into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                    %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}_\mathcal{U}
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \subseteq \Sigma^{'}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is the
            = succeed "-Dedekind" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is the
            = succeed "-Dedekind" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^{'}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq B
            = succeed "-Dedekind" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %h2#lengths_and_angles Lengths and angles
          %p
            Given two points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            of a Euclidean space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x - y
            belongs to the vector space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo =
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x - y\|} = \sqrt{\langle{x - y, x - y}\rangle} .
          %p
            This real number (or properly, element of the line
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %strong distance
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %strong length
            of the line segment
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mi y
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{x y}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            into an (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            \-valued)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
          %p
            Given three points
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \ne z
            (so that
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %mo −
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo ,
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi y
                      %mo −
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x - z\|}, {\|y - z\|} \ne 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mi z
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo −
                        %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi x
                        %mo −
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi y
                        %mo −
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\langle{x - z, y - z}\rangle}{{\|x - z\|} {\|y - z\|}} ,
          %p
            which is a (dimensionless) real number. By the Cauchy–Schwartz inequality, this number lies between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosine"} cosine
            of a unique angle measure between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            radians. This is the measure of the
            %strong angle
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∠
                    %mi x
                    %mi z
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \angle x z y
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∠
                  %mi x
                  %mi z
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \angle x z y
            as a signed angle (so taking values anywhere on the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+circle"} unit circle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            Conversely, knowing angles and lengths, we may recover the inner product on
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo −
                    %mi z
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo −
                    %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mi z
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mi z
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi cos
                  %mo ∠
                  %mi x
                  %mi z
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle{x - z, y - z}\rangle = {\|\overline{x z}\|} {\|\overline{y z}\|} \cos \angle x z y ,
          %p
            and other inner products are recovered by linearity. (We must then use the axioms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+geometry"} Euclidean geometry
            to prove that this is well defined and actually an inner product.) It’s actually possible to recover the inner product and angles from lengths alone; this is discussed at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
